<h1>Plot Window</h1>
<hr align="left" width="100.526%" size="5" class="whs3">
<p><span lang="EN-GB">This command is accessible from</span> <b><span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Reports &gt;</b> Plot Window</span></b> in <span class="Mode" style="text-decoration:underline;"><u>Inspection</u></span> <span lang="EN-US">mode or by pressing</span> <b><i style="font-style:normal; font-weight:bold;"><span class="Button" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Plot Window</b></span> button</i></b> <span lang="EN-US">in the Inspection toolbar.</span></p>
<p class="Description"><b style="font-weight:normal;"><span class="Command" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Description:</b></span> <span class="Command">Plot Window</span></b> <span lang="EN-US">function displays the distribution of the point according to their distance to the reference mesh. Usually this is a Gauss distribution. The width of a single column represents a distance range and the height of a column, the number of the points in this range. Place the mouse cursor for a while over a column and you will see a tooltip with information for the start and the end of the range, as well as number of points that are in this range.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-US">For example on the picture below, the range is from -0.080 to 0.060mm and there are 25049 points inside it.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-US">The <b>Resolution</b> slider defines the width of the lines and accordingly the number of points represented by a line.</span></p>
